{"episodes":{"195536":"A sharp cold front arrived during the early morning hours of October 6th. While the passage was during the overnight hours, a few hundred joules of instability ended up being just enough to aid in stronger winds aloft mixing to the surface. The primary thunder threat with this remained over the eastern U.P. of Michigan into the Tip of the Mitt region prior to 3am ET...with some small hail reported in Sault Ste. Marie, MI with the initial line. Behind this front...winds remained gusty through the rest of the day over much of the region as cooler temperatures overtook the region.","195632":"A surface cold front tracking across Pennsylvania during the afternoon and evening hours on October 6, 2024 in an environment with strong shear and sufficient instability allowed for thunderstorms to form in western Pennsylvania. As these storms moved into central Pennsylvania, they encountered a more stable air mass with less shear but marginally severe thunderstorms continued into portions of Warren and Elk Counties, producing isolated thunderstorm wind damage.","195783":"Scattered thunderstorms developed ahead of an approaching cold front during the early evening hours on Saturday (10/05) and tracked through north-central Wisconsin.  The strongest storms produced hail up to nickel size.","196304":"During the evening hours on October 5th, an isolated storm tracked northeast across southern Taylor County. It went on to produce quarter size hail near Medford.","196583":"A coastal trough along with modest instability nestled along the southern fringes of high pressure extending across the region led to showers and thunderstorms capable of damaging wind gusts.","196672":"The interaction of Hurricane Kirk and another low pressure across the north Atlantic modulated the local weather pattern, pooling abundant moisture and promoting light winds in the region. Additionally, increasing instability due to the proximity of a mid to upper level trough favored deeper convection development each afternoon. This resulted in widespread showers and thunderstorms each day along Puerto Rico, with daily amounts ranging from 2 to 4 inches, and localized higher amounts. With an elevated flooding risk, several flood and landslide reports were received.","196955":"On Sunday October 6th, severe thunderstorms developed within the warm sector of a nearby passing area of low pressure. A pre-frontal trough along with increased instability and shear values caused thunderstorms to develop as the trough tracked into far western New York. As the trough and thunderstorms continued east from near Lake Erie, the increased instability over land, along with around 50 knots of bulk shear, caused the thunderstorms to expand in coverage and strength. Damaging thunderstorm winds and large hail were reported from interior portions of the Western Southern Tier, northeast to near Batavia and to near Rochester. Thunderstorms weakened as they tracked east into the Northern Finger Lakes and points east as instability and shear decreased.","197135":"A cold front moving across the Upper Great Lakes triggered a thunderstorm which produced penny sized hail in Munising on the 5th.","197156":"A cold front moving across the Upper Great Lakes triggered a thunderstorm which produced penny sized hail in Munising on the 5th.","197159":"A strong cold front moving across the area produced a storm force wind gust of 55 mph at Minneapolis Shoal Light late evening on the 5th.","197160":"Lightning caused an attic fire in Marquette during the evening of the 5th as reported by the Marquette Fire Department.  Nearby tree damage was also reported as the lightning strikes were confirmed by NLDN/GLD lightning data sets.","197484":"Strong southwesterly wind ahead of an approaching front eventually advected sufficient moisture and warm air in the lower levels to overcome a capping inversion ahead of a cold front that would eventually be the  main driver of forcing to take advantage of a supportive environment. Damaging wind was the primary threat, although the shear and lapse rates aloft pointed towards a limited potential for large hail as well. Tornadoes looked possible, however, LCLs were relatively high, and any issued TORs did not verify.","197485":"Strong southwesterly wind ahead of an approaching front eventually advected sufficient moisture and warm air in the lower levels to overcome a capping inversion ahead of a cold front that would eventually be the  main driver of forcing to take advantage of a supportive environment. Damaging wind was the primary threat, although the shear and lapse rates aloft pointed towards a limited potential for large hail as well. Tornadoes looked possible, however, LCLs were relatively high, and any issued TORs did not verify.","201787":"During the early afternoon through early evening of October 6th, a surface cold front moved eastward across NE OH and into NW PA. Along the front, a broken line of multicell thunderstorms developed and persisted generally eastward in NE OH. These storms produced sporadic straight-line convective wind damage and marginally-severe hail amidst moderate to strong effective bulk shear, weak to moderate MUCAPE, and moderate DCAPE in the warm and relatively-moist sector.","201790":"During the early afternoon through early evening of October 6th, a surface cold front moved eastward across NE OH and into NW PA. Along the front, a broken line of multicell thunderstorms and isolated supercell thunderstorms developed and persisted generally eastward in NW PA. These storms produced sporadic straight-line convective wind damage and severe hail amidst moderate to strong effective bulk shear, weak to moderate MUCAPE, and moderate DCAPE in the warm and relatively-moist sector."},"events":[{"begin":"2024-10-06T00:46Z","county":"Taylor","damageCrops":"0.00K","damageProperty":"0.00K","deaths":0,"end":"2024-10-06T00:46Z","endLat":45.14,"endLon":-90.35,"episode":196304,"id":1214861,"injuries":0,"lat"